# Line endings
# ============
# Every text file is stored with LF and also checked out with LF, on every
# platform.
#
# `text=auto` alone is not enough. It lets `core.autocrlf` (or the platform
# default) decide the *working tree*, so a Windows checkout would be CRLF while
# a Linux/macOS CI checkout is LF — and `cargo fmt --check`, which is a gate
# (see `docs/ci-operations.md`), compares bytes on disk. The same commit would
# pass on one machine and fail on another. `rustfmt.toml` pins
# `newline_style = "Unix"` for the same reason; the two files are one decision.
#
# The desktop client is developed on Windows, so this is a deliberate trade:
# editors there have to keep LF (which is the default for VS Code, RustRover and
# `rust-analyzer`). The alternative — `eol=crlf` — was measured at 300 changed
# files instead of 60, because the repository is overwhelmingly LF already.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-native command scripts are the one exception: `cmd.exe` mis-parses
# multi-line `if`/`goto` blocks whose lines end in LF. None are tracked today;
# these two lines exist so the first one added does not break the gate.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary assets: never converted, never diffed as text.
*.ico binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary

# Generated files: collapsed in the GitHub diff view and excluded from the
# language statistics, so the mechanical churn of a regeneration does not hide
# the reviewable changes. Keep these paths in sync with the xtask commands that
# write them.
crates/limedl-native/src/i18n/catalog.rs linguist-generated=true
crates/limedl-native/ui/theme.slint linguist-generated=true
